{"name":"Rose Hip","hex":"#fd0e35","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"rose hip","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of rosehip."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/rose_hip"}],"entries":[{"title":"Rose hip","blocks":[{"text":"The rose hip or rosehip, also called rose haw and rose hep, is the accessory fruit of the various species of rose plant. It is typically red to orange, but ranges from dark purple to black in some species. Rose hips begin to form after pollination of flowers in spring or early summer, and ripen in late summer through autumn."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Rose_hips.jpg/330px-Rose_hips.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose_hip"},{"title":"Rosehip neuron","blocks":[{"text":"Rosehip neurons are inhibitory GABAergic neurons present in the first layer of the human cerebral cortex. They make up about 10–15% of all inhibitory neurons in Layer 1. Neurons of this type exist in humans, but have not been reported in rodents."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rosehip_neuron"},{"title":"Rose","blocks":[{"text":"A rose is either a woody perennial flowering plant of the genus Rosa, in the family Rosaceae, or the flower it bears. There are over three hundred species and tens of thousands of cultivars. The plants range in size and form, from trailing and erect shrubs to climbers up to 7 metres (23 ft) in height. Sharp prickles appear on the stems of many species. Their flowers vary in size and shape and are usually large and showy, in colours ranging from white through pinks, reds, oranges and yellows."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e6/Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rose"},{"title":"Hip","blocks":[{"text":"In vertebrate anatomy, the hip, or coxa in medical terminology, refers to either an anatomical region or a joint on the outer (lateral) side of the pelvis."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9f/Hip.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hip"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Rosa_rubiginosa_hips.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_hips.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cd/Rosa_rubiginosa_hips.jpg/330px-Rosa_rubiginosa_hips.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sweet-briar ssp. complete with persistent sepals at the end of the fully ripened hip, backward pointing thorns and hairs covering the pedicels and fruiting body.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARosa_rubiginosa_hips.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Rosa_roxburghii_-_Quarryhill_Botanical_Garden_-_DSC03252.JPG/500px-Rosa_roxburghii_-_Quarryhill_Botanical_Garden_-_DSC03252.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Rosa_roxburghii_-_Quarryhill_Botanical_Garden_-_DSC03252.JPG/1280px-Rosa_roxburghii_-_Quarryhill_Botanical_Garden_-_DSC03252.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/89/Rosa_roxburghii_-_Quarryhill_Botanical_Garden_-_DSC03252.JPG/500px-Rosa_roxburghii_-_Quarryhill_Botanical_Garden_-_DSC03252.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Rose hips from Rosa roxburghii in Quarryhill Botanical Garden, California ","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ARosa_roxburghii_-_Quarryhill_Botanical_Garden_-_DSC03252.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wi